+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole number
+of seconds, like `90`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes),
+and `s` (seconds). Each unit may be used at most once, in that order, without spaces, like `2d`, `1h30m`, or
+`1d2h3m4s`. Units are case-insensitive. Negative numbers, decimals, and any other format are rejected.
+
+A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` given as a default or passed from Python is used as-is.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
